What you can do

* Draw directly with your finger, or use arrows and circles.
* Color-code aching, radiating pain, numbness and scars.
* Pinch-zoom, move the image, erase, undo and redo.
* Save a marked-up PNG at original resolution.
* Export editable JSON coordinates for the Pantheon to interpret later.

The reusable version contains no personal photo, so that is the version to put in a public repository.

Put it in your Pantheon repo

On your iPhone:

* Save the reusable HTML file above.
* In GitHub, open your Pantheon repository and choose Add file → Upload files.
* Upload it to the repository root as pain-map.html.
* Commit it. You can enable GitHub Pages to run it in Safari at https://USERNAME.github.io/REPO/pain-map.html.

If ChatGPT’s file preview doesn’t execute interactive HTML, GitHub Pages is the more reliable way to run it on your phone.

I also recommend adding the module seed to the repo. Its core implementation instruction is:

# PANTHEON — Visual Annotation Operator
Build a reusable, touch-first image annotation interface.
Input: Any user-supplied image.
Interaction: Draw directly on the image with a finger
or stylus. Support circles, arrows, freehand paths,
eraser, zoom, pan, undo and redo.
Preserve marks as normalized image coordinates.
Separate original pixels from user annotations.
Export both a flattened PNG and structured JSON.
Allow importing JSON to restore editable marks.
Transduction:
Image → User observation → Spatial coordinates →
Semantic labels → Hypotheses → Verification.
Applications:
Medical pain maps, field observations, object
identification, image forensics, and Pantheon Painter.
Never silently turn a user's mark into a confirmed
medical diagnosis. Preserve uncertainty and provenance.
Make the interface work on iPhone, offline,
without external dependencies.

Testing: I verified the photo loads, drawing and arrows work, undo/redo works, zoom resets, PNG exports at the original 1080 × 1440 resolution, and exported JSON restores editable marks. I haven’t tested it on your actual iPhone yet.

Once you’ve drawn the red area where the ankle aches and the orange path showing how far it travels, send me the annotated image. That will give us much more precise information than guessing from where your finger is pointing.

And this can become a general Pantheon operator: point to meaning, preserve its coordinates, and let different systems interpret it without losing the original observation.